.{
    .name = .pabrikcore,
    .version = "0.0.0",
    .fingerprint = 0x3c325c50dfa3ce66,
    .minimum_zig_version = "0.16.0",
    .dependencies = .{
        // Self-contained sqlite3 / openssl / libpq package (ruangsql).
        // Pinned by URL + hash; exposes a single `databases` module;
        // consumers `@import("databases")` to use it.
        .databases = .{
            // 2b17251 = branch `fix/fd-headroom-scales-with-budget`
            // (ruangsql#9): the reader pool's ceiling no longer collapses to 1
            // when the process's soft `RLIMIT_NOFILE` is at or below
            // `FD_HEADROOM`. The reserve is now `min(FD_HEADROOM, soft / 2)`,
            // so macOS's default of 256 funds 64 readers instead of 1, while
            // every budget at or above 512 keeps the ceiling it had.
            //
            // WHAT THAT COST BEFORE THIS PIN. macOS hands a process 256, which
            // IS `FD_HEADROOM`, so the ceiling evaluated to 1 — the elastic
            // pool could never open a second reader past its warm floor, and
            // every read that found that one reader busy waited out the whole
            // `reader_wait_ms` budget:
            //
            //   warning: sqlite: reader pool unavailable after waiting 5000ms
            //            (PoolExhausted); serving this read on the write
            //            connection
            //
            // Measured (isolated HOME, `ulimit -S -n 256`):
            // `GET /api/llm/session` 5.001 s x4 with `fd_backstop=1`; the same
            // request is sub-millisecond at 262+. The regression test for it is
            // `tests/functional/fd_limit_reader_pool_test.zig`.
            //
            // This is a BRANCH SHA, not main: move it to the merge SHA once #9
            // lands.
            //
            // PREVIOUS PIN, kept because both essays below still describe the
            // trap that makes moving this pin dangerous —
            // 4667a64 = branch `fix/pool-wait-instead-of-hijack-writer`
            // (ruangsql#6), "a read waits for a reader instead of taking the
            // write connection", plus the idle reaper (8dbfd61) and "a
            // reaping pass must never orphan a slot" (4667a64) that came
            // with it. This is a BRANCH SHA, not main: main's #5 merge is
            // `fd52da1`, and none of the above is on it yet. Move this pin to
            // the merge SHA once #6 lands.
            //
            // WHY THIS SHA IS NOT e7cfb3f, which #851 pinned. #851 was merged
            // from the branch's PRE-rebase state, so main went in with a SHA
            // that no longer exists on any branch. GitHub still served it, so
            // main kept building — against the code as it was before the
            // rebase, i.e. without the orphan fix and without
            // `outstandingClaims`. That is the RE-PINNING trap below in its
            // purest form: the build was green and the pin was wrong.
            //
            // Two things to check when this pin moves, because neither is
            // implied by a successful build:
            //   1. `.hash` CHANGED when the content changed, so a hash that
            //      does not move across a SHA bump means the URL did not move
            //      either (the hash is derived from the extracted content).
            //   2. `rm -rf zig-pkg` before believing it. Zig keys the package
            //      cache by `.hash`, so a stale extract satisfies a build
            //      after the URL changes.
            //
            // WHAT #6 FIXES HERE, since the symptom arrived as a log line and
            // not as a bug report:
            //
            //     warning: sqlite: reader pool unavailable (PoolExhausted);
            //              serving this read on the write connection
            //
            // repeated once per read, for every read in a burst. `claimReader`
            // fell back to the PRIMARY connection, which carries one mutex and
            // the WAL's single writer slot — so the read serialized against
            // every concurrent write AND held the writer mutex for its whole
            // duration, delaying unrelated `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`s into their own
            // 15 s `busy_timeout`. On a 4 GiB `agent.db` that is the
            // difference between slow and `database is locked`. A read now
            // waits for a pooled reader (`Config.reader_wait_ms`, default 5 s)
            // and only falls back when the pool stays saturated for the whole
            // budget; the fallback is logged at most once per minute.
            //
            // The reaper (49ac34f) is what makes "elastic" mean transient:
            // before it, `claim` opened a reader per in-flight read and
            // NOTHING ever closed one except `closeAll` at shutdown, so peak
            // concurrency set the connection count for the life of the process
            // (75 readers = 150 fds observed while developing it).
            //
            // Kept for reference — fd52da1 = merge of ginwa123/ruangsql#5,
            // "stop the reader pool from exhausting file descriptors", which
            // introduced the fd-derived ceiling and `PoolExhausted` in the
            // first place. #842 asked for an unlimited reader pool
            // (`max_read_conns = 0`). Taken literally that is unbounded, and
            // it exhausted this daemon's descriptors: one fd per connected SSE
            // client, plus two per pooled sqlite reader. Observed as
            // `unable to open database file (sql: PRAGMA journal_mode =
            // WAL)` — `sqlite3_open` returning SQLITE_CANTOPEN, surfaced
            // from a PRAGMA, so it reads like a permissions problem and is
            // neither — and then a heap-corrupting double free on that same
            // error path (`panic: incorrect alignment`). #5 caps the pool at
            // the process's real `RLIMIT_NOFILE` minus headroom for the
            // app's own sockets, and removes the double free.
            //
            // #5 also carries what the previous pin was reaching for. The
            // pin used to be `389dfad`, which is #4's PRE-merge SHA — kept
            // because #3's pooled readers merged alone are BROKEN for this
            // app: #3 unified the empty-slice binding of `exec` with
            // `query`/`queryRow`, silently turning the `""` optional filter
            // into SQL NULL and making three `skill_evals_db` tests
            // (`listRuns` uses `WHERE (? = '' OR id = ?)`) return no rows.
            // #4 restored the read-side binding and is now `a39c15f` on
            // main, so that reason to pin a pre-merge SHA is gone.
            //
            // Kept for reference — 46d96dd = merge of ginwa123/ruangsql#1,
            // "take the WAL writer slot up front instead of losing writes"
            // — busy_timeout 5s -> 15s, a real Config, `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` in
            // `begin()` so a read-then-write transaction cannot hit an
            // instant, unretryable SQLITE_BUSY_SNAPSHOT, and
            // `database.openWithConfig` so this app can set its own
            // `synchronous`. Before it, a contended write FAILED with
            // "database is locked" and was lost.
            //
            // RE-PINNING: this URL is a COMMIT SHA. A squash-merge of
            // ruangsql invalidates it — the old SHA stays reachable on its
            // branch so the URL keeps resolving, and the build keeps
            // SUCCEEDING against the pre-merge code. Zig also keys the
            // package cache by `.hash`, not by `.url`, so changing the URL
            // alone changes nothing. To move the pin: take the new SHA from
            // `git -C ../ruangsql rev-parse origin/main`, put it here, put
            // a throwaway in `.hash`, run `zig build`, and paste in the
            // `hash mismatch: ... but the fetched package has ...` value it
            // prints. Then `rm -rf zig-pkg && zig build` and confirm the
            // new symbol you rely on actually exists in the extracted
            // package.
            .url = "git+https://github.com/ginwa123/ruangsql.git#2b17251898b78eb879fb0b09dc1f2968ee0200ef",
            .hash = "databases-0.0.0-vy-BCE1qBgCG8uwiEeCykTNb8j63ENIeS4VJAuAtnRXb",
        },
        // External web-framework library (pure-Zig HTTP server +
        // libcurl-backed HTTP client). Exposes a single `kabelweb` module
        // — consumers `@import("kabelweb")` and reach
        // `kabelweb.server` / `kabelweb.client`.
        //
        // LOCAL DEV OVERRIDE: this is the sibling checkout so the SSE fixes
        // developed in ../kabelweb are exercised here immediately (notably
        // commit 7e97a09 — "keep SSE clients registered after the handler
        // returns + real loop threads", which stops /api/events from
        // delivering a single `connected` frame and then going silent).
        //
        // To restore the released/pinned dependency (CI, other machines),
        // swap this back to:
        //   .url = "git+https://github.com/ginwa123/kabelweb.git#<commit>",
        //   .hash = "kabelweb-0.0.0-<hash>",
        // after pushing the kabelweb commit you want to pin.
        .kabelweb = .{
            .url = "git+https://github.com/ginwa123/kabelweb.git#02cc37f7cd064a94fe6385d293969d6cc3220bfd",
            .hash = "kabelweb-0.0.0-vhZam5cXHQDuXHyFVwHUaROmfGKA69u4F-Spzbc3btsU",
        },
        // Cross-platform portable helpers (sleep, fileExists,
        // unixTimestampNanos, etc.) used by every module. Promoted
        // to its own package so multiple sub-packages can `b.dependency`
        // it without each creating a separate `helpers` module that
        // would collide on the file's module ownership.
        .helpers = .{
            .path = "src/helpers",
        },
        // The Zig functional test suite (tests/functional/). A SEPARATE
        // package with an EMPTY dependency list of its own: it boots the
        // `pabrik` binary as a child process and drives it over HTTP, and
        // must never be able to `@import` a `src/` internal - a test that
        // bound to an internal type could pass without crossing the wire,
        // which is the one class of test this suite exists to rule out.
        .functional_tests = .{
            .path = "tests/functional",
        },
    },
    .paths = .{
        "build.zig",
        "build.zig.zon",
        "src",
        // The functional test package is a path dependency, so it must be
        // inside the package hash.
        "tests",
        // Exclude vendored deps from the package hash — they're
        // hermetic (sqlite3 amalgamation is deterministic) but bloat
        // the cache. Same convention as kabelweb/build.zig.zon.
        // (Doesn't matter much for in-tree path deps, but keeps the
        // hash consistent with future url-based consumers.)
    },
}
